  "worked_solution": "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{\u2015}{x},\\overset{\u2015}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\u222c}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\u222c}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\u222c}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\u222c}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}x\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x(\\frac{x}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2})}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=2.\n$$",
